### Corrections made in this version
- The schema was written in SQLite types. It is now in Postgres types, with UUID keys, `timestamptz`, and CHECK constraints in place of SQLite TEXT conventions.
- Business rule on lockout said a locked account returns at `locked_until`, which contradicted admin-only unlock. The users table now uses `is_locked` and `locked_at`.
- The `shifts` table and the `shift_id` column on care assignments were optional and unlinked, so handover had no table. Shifts are now the dose time windows, and handover notes have their own table.
- The "existing sessions end" rule had no table to enforce it. A `sessions` table was added.
- Temporary passwords had no expiry. They now expire after 7 days.
- Medicine approval had no status column. `approval_status` was added.
- Lab uploads had no table. `resident_documents` was added.
- The dose unit list (mg, ml, tablet, IU) conflicted with the stock-unit rule. A dose unit must now equal the medicine's stock unit.
- Doctors were shown as viewers of all residents. Doctors now see only their own residents.
- The earlier login design showed the code field to everyone. The code step now appears only after a correct password for an MFA account.
- Caretakers' stock adjustment rights were marked "to be decided". They can now propose adjustments, which admins approve.
- Audit log was described as append-only without enforcement. A trigger now enforces it.
- Shift summaries were implied to go to the AI assistant. The app now calculates them.
- The credential file was described as shown once without saying it cannot be downloaded again. This is now stated.
- Allergy severity was recorded, but no rule used it. Severe allergies now block prescribing.
- Login history recorded IP addresses with no retention limit. A 365-day limit was added.
- Rules that depended on undecided settings were given defaults, so none remain open.